WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.720
In the 1840s, a hospital in Vienna had two maternity clinics.

2
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.420
Year after year, a much larger share of mothers died of childbed fever in the First

3
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:14.620
Clinic than in the Second.

4
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:19.080
Childbed fever is a serious infection after giving birth.

5
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.100
How did we figure out why?

6
00:00:22.083 --> 00:00:25.463
The First Clinic trained doctors, who often worked with bodies.

7
00:00:26.343 --> 00:00:27.843
The Second Clinic trained midwives.

8
00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:34.763
In 1847, Semmelweis began washing hands in chlorine, and the death ratio, the share

9
00:00:34.763 --> 00:00:36.043
of mothers who died, fell.

10
00:00:37.223 --> 00:00:38.723
Germ theory later explained why.

11
00:00:39.563 --> 00:00:41.983
It says many diseases are caused by microbes.

12
00:00:42.863 --> 00:00:46.263
A microbe is a living thing too small to see without a microscope.

13
00:00:47.043 --> 00:00:49.623
A microbe that causes disease is a pathogen.

14
00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:55.877
A vaccine teaches the immune system to recognize a pathogen without causing the full

15
00:00:55.877 --> 00:01:01.157
disease. It works through an antigen, a substance the immune system can recognize.

16
00:01:01.997 --> 00:01:05.417
The body makes antibodies, proteins that stick to one antigen.

17
00:01:06.237 --> 00:01:09.277
The first time, the response is slow, often a week or more.

18
00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:15.610
During that first response, some immune cells become memory cells.

19
00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.610
Memory cells stay in the body long after.

20
00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:25.110
On a later exposure, memory cells recognize the antigen quickly, and memory B cells

21
00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.690
turn into cells that make large amounts of antibody.

22
00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:33.050
The vaccine gave the first meeting, so the real pathogen meets antibody already

23
00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:34.710
waiting and a faster response.

24
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.960
An antibiotic is a drug that kills bacteria or stops them growing.

25
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.300
Many antibiotics block the building of the bacterial cell wall.

26
00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.640
Others jam bacterial ribosomes, the parts that make proteins.

27
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.820
A virus has no cell wall and no ribosomes of its own.

28
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.660
It uses the ribosomes of the cell it infects.

29
00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.260
So antibiotics do not treat colds or flu.

30
00:02:00.283 --> 00:02:06.203
Antibiotic resistance is the ability of some bacteria to survive a drug that kills

31
00:02:06.203 --> 00:02:07.283
others of their kind.

32
00:02:08.223 --> 00:02:12.523
In a large population, a few may already carry a gene that lets them survive.

33
00:02:13.303 --> 00:02:16.203
The antibiotic kills or stops the sensitive ones.

34
00:02:16.983 --> 00:02:19.143
The resistant ones are left to multiply.

35
00:02:20.043 --> 00:02:24.823
This is natural selection: individuals with a helpful trait survive and reproduce

36
00:02:24.823 --> 00:02:27.583
more. So why do they not always succeed?

37
00:02:27.583 --> 00:02:33.603
Vaccines do not work perfectly in everyone, and resistant bacteria survive the drug.
